  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "claim_fc476b2349fe459f930bbc68c1972b49",
      "statement": "Brijio's core trust assumptions are that the user explicitly starts and controls the browser connection, authentication tokens are held only by authorized parties, the browser session belongs to the user, and the relay transports messages without needing to inspect content; these are non-negotiable product invariants that browser state is available only while the user-controlled extension is connected and every read or action is initiated by an explicit MCP request with no continuous streaming.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://AGENTS.md#L38-L60",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:04ca3080466731e8f9b61dfb7de334f59f0e1af72138e5e9c570f2c25a692657:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/THREAT_MODEL.md#L58-L63",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f631d289d658d40bca434b52ed9ebf9309f2fba350ff5bc3b1f30ff4c54bab9a:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_42a60c4d2fc041e6b328eb46f495f504",
      "statement": "The trust boundaries are token-gated: the agent is not trusted (MCP HTTP requires a Bearer token and optional origin allowlist), the MCP server and extension both authenticate to the relay with the same pairing token but in roles 'mcp' and 'extension', the relay routes between authenticated parties without needing payload inspection, and the extension reads browser content as structured data without trusting or evaluating web page content.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/TRUST_BOUNDARIES.md#L11-L34",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:080a8d639bf2e2a0cbab71c0dba45764e9e803ac2536da4bfb6b1801b802ec01:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://servers/mcp/src/http-server.ts#L167-L183",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:68a4949a3f09c44b89db3bb13248c656c363fa44fd879884f6daddf2defa4fa3:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://servers/websocket/src/server.ts#L164-L201",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:b46feefbac4ae93b0b894a224e4f83269648c24c8e0a6ead3434ca3a353bb2fb:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e765e70ebffe43268159d348720d3852",
      "statement": "Brijio's security goals are to protect browser sessions, avoid credential exposure, minimize unnecessary data transfer, reduce trust in relay infrastructure, and maintain user control; the mitigations against malicious-agent, compromised-relay, network-attacker, and malicious-website threats are architectural (explicit request/response, no streaming, user control, progressive disclosure, least-privilege permissions, read-not-evaluated content).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/THREAT_MODEL.md#L15-L56",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:516eaac0b5bd356695be1732f5b0f14fe10cf05445fbbb54efc7f264e340498f:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/security/THREAT_MODEL.md#L8-L13",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:04d82b3fc72188bdc94c71600111ab9c3afd4fcaa707ba3330c28e846a817c18:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_4995bec8c36a4855bfb2b76f8e676108",
      "statement": "The relay authenticates each connection against the pairing token set (BRIJIO_PAIRING_TOKEN plus optional additionalPairingTokens), rejects unknown tokens with auth_failed, and derives a sha256 scope key from the accepted token; presence and pending-request routing are keyed by scope so browsers and requests under different tokens are isolated.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://servers/websocket/src/protocol.ts#L16-L18",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a8f99f02da218cabd6e95c9cb7ffc963fa0b303b84f2c3a84212e4155ca2c78e:"
        },
